been regarded as the man who completed the conquest of what would later be Brega for the descendants of Niall of the Nine Hostages.", "newValue": "Túathal mac Cormaic, known to history as Túathal Máelgarb, emerged as a pivotal High King of Ireland in the early 6th century, reigning until his death in 544 CE. As a purported grandson of Coirpre mac Néill, a son of the legendary Niall of the Nine Hostages, Túathal Máelgarb's significance lies in his critical role in consolidating the nascent power of the Uí Néill dynasty, particularly in the fertile and strategic lands of what would later become Brega. His reign marked a decisive phase in the Uí Néill's expansion from their western midland strongholds, cementing their claim to paramountcy over numerous provincial kingdoms.

The core achievement attributed to Túathal Máelgarb was the completion of the conquest and subjugation of territories east of the River Shannon, specifically the region of Brega in modern County Meath. This involved overcoming resistance from indigenous tribal groups and establishing a permanent Uí Néill presence, transforming this rich agricultural area into a dynastic heartland. While details of specific battles are scant in the earliest annals, the narrative emphasizes his forceful assertion of authority, which effectively pushed the Uí Néill frontier eastward and secured vital resources and strategic access for future generations of High Kings, notably those of the Southern Uí Néill.

The long-term legacy of Túathal Máelgarb's reign was profound for the political geography of early medieval Ireland. By solidifying Uí Néill control over Brega, he provided a robust territorial and economic base that would sustain the Southern Uí Néill's ascendancy for centuries, making them the most powerful sept in the island's political landscape. This territorial consolidation was instrumental in shaping the trajectory of the High Kingship, moving it towards a more centralized and dynastic institution, even if its actual power remained fluid and contested among various Uí Néill branches."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-04T07:06:52Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#37184262858", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The aggressive expansion of the Uí Néill dynasty from their initial power base in the Irish midlands created a need for territorial consolidation.", "The fragmented political landscape of early medieval Ireland, characterized by numerous petty kingdoms, offered opportunities for ambitious regional powers like the Uí Néill to assert dominance.", "The strategic and economic importance of the fertile plains of Brega fueled Uí Néill ambitions to control this vital agricultural region."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-04T07:06:52Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#37184262858", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "544 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "544 CE"}], "newValue": ["Túathal Máelgarb's reign led to the decisive consolidation of Uí Néill power over the strategically vit